WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.738
Welcome back to your Danish citizenship exam companion.

2
00:00:03.688 --> 00:00:11.544
This is Chapter Six, Episode Sixteen: discrimination, antisemitism, hate crimes and extremism.

3
00:00:12.094 --> 00:00:17.069
Denmark is built on ideals of, among other things, freedom and democracy.

4
00:00:17.019 --> 00:00:21.569
These ideals are laid down in Denmark's constitution, grundloven.

5
00:00:21.569 --> 00:00:27.819
The constitution for example determines that you can say or write what you want, freedom of expression.

6
00:00:27.819 --> 00:00:33.231
The constitution also determines that you can choose the religion you wish, freedom of religion.

7
00:00:33.231 --> 00:00:43.626
The constitution also protects the right to form associations, freedom of association, and to assemble, for example for demonstrations, freedom of assembly.

8
00:00:44.176 --> 00:00:50.214
But it is illegal to misuse the freedom for certain forms of discrimination, diskrimination.

9
00:00:50.164 --> 00:01:01.801
There is discrimination if you treat people differently and worse because of their faith, political conviction, skin colour, sex, ethnicity, sexual orientation, age and so on.

10
00:01:01.801 --> 00:01:06.001
People often say that "freedom must be used with responsibility".

11
00:01:06.001 --> 00:01:14.220
In general, ideals of equality and democracy have high support in Denmark, but there are also examples of discrimination.

12
00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:25.645
Studies indicate that almost half of all citizens with a non-Western immigrant background in Denmark have experienced being discriminated against in some way because of their ethnic origin.

13
00:01:25.595 --> 00:01:37.590
Studies show that applicants with certain foreign names have a harder time getting a rented home or being invited to a job interview, even if they are just as qualified as the applicants with typically Danish names.

14
00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:50.112
You also must not publicly say or write anything that degrades, ridicules or threatens others because of, for example, their race, skin colour, faith or sexual orientation.

15
00:01:50.662 --> 00:02:00.812
Likewise it is illegal to use your freedom of expression to publicly call for a crime to be committed, for example by calling for violence against a particular group of people.

16
00:02:00.762 --> 00:02:06.570
You must also not publicly express your clear support for a terror attack that has already been committed.

17
00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:15.795
Many of these rules are made to protect religious, ethnic and sexual minorities against abuse from the state and against abuse from other citizens.

18
00:02:15.745 --> 00:02:26.595
To protect minorities you can thus punish crimes particularly severely if they are committed because of the victim's ethnic origin, faith, sexual orientation or similar.

19
00:02:26.595 --> 00:02:30.588
Such crimes are also called hate crimes, hadforbrydelser.

20
00:02:31.138 --> 00:02:39.813
In the period 2022 to 24, the police in Denmark registered 2,108 cases of hate crimes.

21
00:02:39.763 --> 00:02:57.913
1,022 of them, 48 percent, were because of the victims' race or ethnicity, 756, 36 percent, because of the victims' religion, and 435, 21 percent, because of the victims' sexual orientation.

22
00:02:57.913 --> 00:03:03.576
The crimes are typically hateful statements, violence, vandalism or threats.

23
00:03:03.576 --> 00:03:10.614
This corresponds to 702 hate crimes on average per year in the period, or about two cases every day.

24
00:03:11.164 --> 00:03:17.226
The Jewish minority in Denmark is an example of a group that is particularly exposed to hate crimes.

25
00:03:17.177 --> 00:03:24.289
In the period 2022 to 24, the police registered 430 hate crimes against Jews.

26
00:03:24.289 --> 00:03:30.164
That is a high number considering that only a total of 6,000 to 8,000 Jews live in Denmark.

27
00:03:30.164 --> 00:03:40.632
More than half of the hate crimes committed in Denmark because of the victim's religion, and more than every fifth of all hate crimes in Denmark, were committed against Jews.

28
00:03:41.182 --> 00:03:51.195
For example, gravestones in a Jewish cemetery in Randers in 2019 were doused with paint and knocked over by men from a neo-Nazi movement.

29
00:03:51.144 --> 00:04:05.370
It happened symbolically on the 9th of November, that is on the anniversary of Kristallnacht in 1938, when the Nazis, among other things, smashed and burned Jewish shops, synagogues, homes and schools in Germany.

30
00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:16.233
After the conflict between Israel and Hamas began in October 2023, Jewish citizens in Denmark increasingly experienced threats and harassment.

31
00:04:16.183 --> 00:04:29.870
Much of this has taken place online, for example via social media such as Facebook, Instagram and X, where they have, among other things, been held responsible for Israel's policy towards the Palestinians.

32
00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:35.670
In several cases this has happened with hateful comments and Nazi symbols such as the swastika.

33
00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:50.172
On the 5th of May 2023, that is the anniversary of Denmark's liberation in 1945, a memorial stone in Copenhagen for the Danes' help for the Danish Jews during the Second World War was doused with red paint.

34
00:04:50.722 --> 00:05:04.684
The most serious example of an antisemitic act in Denmark in recent times is probably the terror attack in 2015, where a terrorist with sympathy for the terror organisation Islamic State shot and killed a Jewish guard.

35
00:05:04.634 --> 00:05:10.206
It happened when the terrorist in vain tried to force his way into the synagogue in central Copenhagen.

36
00:05:10.756 --> 00:05:14.218
There are also examples of hate crimes against other groups.

37
00:05:14.168 --> 00:05:28.468
For example, a Danish party leader was in 2020 convicted for, in a video, saying that "the Danes and Denmark are far too highly developed and advanced to be able to coexist with the very low-grade Muslim cultural rubbish".

38
00:05:28.468 --> 00:05:40.993
And in 2025, three persons were sentenced to between six and ten months in prison for aggravated violence after having assaulted, hit, kicked and stamped on the heads of two homosexual men.

39
00:05:40.993 --> 00:05:46.320
One of the men was also convicted for having filmed the incident and shared it on social media.

40
00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:58.470
In 2015, Nazi graffiti with threats was painted and a car was set on fire at an asylum centre by a perpetrator who was an opponent of the Danish refugee policy.

41
00:05:58.420 --> 00:06:02.514
The perpetrator was sentenced to one and a half years in prison.

42
00:06:03.064 --> 00:06:08.839
At other times crimes are committed because the perpetrators do not like the victim's political views.

43
00:06:08.789 --> 00:06:21.526
In 2003 the then prime minister Anders Fogh Rasmussen and foreign minister Per Stig Møller were attacked with paint in the Folketing because of Denmark's support for the USA's invasion of Iraq, the Iraq War.

44
00:06:21.526 --> 00:06:26.564
The two perpetrators were sentenced to three and four months in prison respectively.

45
00:06:26.564 --> 00:06:31.836
The courts thought it was particularly serious that they had attacked representatives of democracy.

46
00:06:32.386 --> 00:06:45.366
In recent decades the Police Intelligence Service, PET, has had to protect an increasing number of politicians with bodyguards, because PET assesses that more politicians are threatened by attacks or harassment.

47
00:06:45.916 --> 00:06:48.428
Here is your recap for Episode Sixteen.

48
00:06:48.378 --> 00:06:54.428
The constitution protects freedom of expression, religion, association and assembly.

49
00:06:54.428 --> 00:07:05.203
Discrimination is treating people worse because of faith, political conviction, skin colour, sex, ethnicity, sexual orientation, age and so on.

50
00:07:05.203 --> 00:07:18.066
You must not publicly degrade, ridicule or threaten others because of race, skin colour, faith or sexual orientation, nor call for crimes or support a terror attack that has been committed.

51
00:07:18.066 --> 00:07:27.278
Crimes committed because of the victim's ethnic origin, faith or sexual orientation are hate crimes and can be punished more severely.

52
00:07:27.278 --> 00:07:42.928
In 2022 to 24, the police registered 2,108 hate crimes: 48 percent for race or ethnicity, 36 percent for religion, 21 percent for sexual orientation; about two cases a day.

53
00:07:42.928 --> 00:07:49.116
430 were against Jews, though only 6,000 to 8,000 Jews live in Denmark.

54
00:07:49.116 --> 00:07:59.278
The 2015 terror attack, at the Jewish synagogue in central Copenhagen, killed a Jewish guard outside it; it was not at the central station or the airport.

55
00:07:59.278 --> 00:08:07.391
In 2003 the prime minister Anders Fogh Rasmussen and the foreign minister Per Stig Møller were attacked with paint in the Folketing.

56
00:08:07.391 --> 00:08:10.416
PET protects an increasing number of politicians.

57
00:08:10.966 --> 00:08:13.566
Now it is time for your exam practice.

58
00:08:13.516 --> 00:08:18.428
These are real questions from past Danish citizenship exams on what you just heard.

59
00:08:18.428 --> 00:08:25.914
For each one, I will read the question and the choices, then pause so you can pick your answer, and then I will give you the correct answer.

60
00:08:26.464 --> 00:08:27.714
Question 1.

61
00:08:27.664 --> 00:08:32.564
At which place in Copenhagen was a terror attack carried out in 2015?

62
00:08:32.564 --> 00:08:35.114
Option A: At the Jewish synagogue.

63
00:08:35.114 --> 00:08:37.651
Option B: At the Central Station.

64
00:08:37.651 --> 00:08:39.756
Option C: At the airport.
[DA] Hvilket af følgende steder i København blev der begået et terrorangreb i 2015?  A: Ved den jødiske synagoge  |  B: Ved Hovedbanegården  |  C: I lufthavnen

65
00:08:46.306 --> 00:08:48.906
The answer is A: At the Jewish synagogue.

66
00:08:48.856 --> 00:08:51.510
It appeared in the May 2023 exam.
[DA] ✔ A: Ved den jødiske synagoge

67
00:08:52.060 --> 00:08:53.822
That's the end of this part.

68
00:08:53.772 --> 00:08:54.840
Well done.

